~ start
Prime Minister Terborg: We eat on the water so that nobody can leave early. Tonight the subject is the rules of the digital economy, and I intend to win.
- Back a European digital levy => levy
- Back light-touch regulation => light
- Raise the Amsterdam tax rulings => rulings
~ levy
Prime Minister Terborg: A levy on the giants. Nice idea, stalled in every capital, by me among others.
- Push for a one percent levy => one_pct
- Push for a smaller, quicker one => smaller
~ one_pct
Prime Minister Terborg: One percent. The giants will lobby, my ministry will lobby harder against it, and the boat will not sink.
=> END
~ smaller
Prime Minister Terborg: Smaller, quicker. A quick compromise is a rare bird in these waters.
=> END
~ light
Prime Minister Terborg: Light touch. Europeans regulate first and innovate later. I would reverse the order for at least one cycle.
- Offer a regulatory sandbox => sandbox
- Offer an innovation fund => innov
~ sandbox
Prime Minister Terborg: A sandbox. Startups love sand, and so do lawyers, who sell shovels.
=> END
~ innov
Prime Minister Terborg: An innovation fund. Money in a fund is a promise. Let us keep it away from consultants.
=> END
~ rulings
Prime Minister Terborg: Our tax rulings have been called a loophole. I prefer to call them a competitive advantage that the Commission dislikes.
- Demand they be ended => ended
- Accept them if profits are taxed where earned => earned
~ ended
Prime Minister Terborg: Ended? The Treasury will weep, and the city of The Hague will ask who forgot to thank it.
=> END
~ earned
Prime Minister Terborg: Where earned. A reasonable compromise, and an expensive one for several famous companies.
=> END
